<h2>How the Throughput Calculator works</h2><p>Throughput is the rate at which a process produces completed good output over a defined time window. This calculator uses throughput = good units completed ÷ productive time. It also normalizes the result to an hourly rate so teams can compare windows expressed in minutes, hours or days without changing the underlying production count.</p><h2>How to use this throughput calculator</h2><p>Enter the number of good units completed, the productive time represented by that count and the matching time unit. Add a target rate if you want a positive or negative gap. Use the same inclusion rules each time: for example, decide whether breaks, planned changeovers and blocked time belong inside or outside the productive-time denominator.</p><h2>How to interpret the result</h2><p>A higher rate means more good output is flowing through the measured process in the same amount of productive time. The target gap is useful for daily management because it shows the rate difference directly, but it does not explain the cause. Pair the result with downtime, quality, changeover and bottleneck data before deciding what to improve.</p><h2>Assumptions and limitations</h2><p>Throughput is definition-sensitive. Counting started units, total units including scrap, or shipments instead of completed good units produces different metrics. A short observation window can also exaggerate the effect of batch releases or temporary stoppages. This tool does not infer cycle time, WIP, OEE or capacity from throughput alone.</p><h2>Practical example and workflow</h2><p>If a line completes 1,200 good units in 8 productive hours, throughput is 150 units/hour. Against a 140-unit/hour target, the line is 10 units/hour above target. A supervisor can then check whether that improvement came from less downtime, a faster constraint operation or an unusual product mix.</p>
